Q1. What is Java?
Java is a high-level, object-oriented, platform-independent programming language.
Note:- 
-> High-level : High-level languages use syntax that is easier for humans to read and write, like English-like keywords (if, for, class, etc.).
                Example: System.out.println("Hello"); is readable to humans, whereas machine code (10101010...) is not.
-> platform-independent : Java programs are platform-independent because the bytecode runs on any OS via JVM, so you don’t have to rewrite code for different systems.


Q2. What are the features of Java? 
     -> Object-Oriented – Based on classes and objects for modularity.
     -> Platform-Independent – Write once, run anywhere using JVM.
     -> Robust – Strong memory management and error handling.
     -> Portable – Can run across devices and OS without changes.
     -> High Performance – Uses JIT compiler for faster execution.
     -> Multithreaded – Supports multiple threads for concurrent tasks.

Q3. DIFFERENCE BETWEEN JVM,JRE AND JDK ?
| Component | What it is                                     | Contains                                                                |
| --------- | ---------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **JVM**   | Java Virtual Machine, runs bytecode            | Just the JVM itself (executes `.class` files)                           |
| **JRE**   | Java Runtime Environment, runs Java apps       | **JVM + Core Java Libraries** (like `java.lang`, `java.util`)           |
| **JDK**   | Java Development Kit, for developing Java apps | **JRE + Development Tools** (`javac`, `jar`, `javadoc`, debugger, etc.) |


