Q11. Difference between this keyword and super keyword ?
| Point                     | `this` keyword                                                | `super` keyword                                                       |
| ------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **1. Refers to**          | Current class object (me)                                     | Parent class object (parent)                                          |
| **2. Used for variables** | To access current class variables                             | To access parent class variables                                      |
| **3. Used for methods**   | To call current class methods                                 | To call parent class methods (when overridden)                        |
| **4. Constructor call**   | Calls another constructor in the same class (`this()`)        | Calls parent class constructor (`super()`)                            |
| **5. Ambiguity**          | Clears confusion between local variable and instance variable | Clears confusion when child and parent have same variable/method name |

Q12. Explain instanceof keyword.
Checks whether an object is an instance of a specific class or subclass.
Example :
specific class :-
 List<Object> list = new ArrayList<>();
        list.add("Java"); 
        list.add(100); 
        list.add(new Date());

        for (Object obj : list) {
            if (obj instanceof String) {
                System.out.println(obj + " is a String");
            } else if (obj instanceof Integer) {
                System.out.println(obj + " is an Integer");
            } else {
                System.out.println(obj + " is some other type");
            }
subclass :- 
class Animal {}
class Dog extends Animal {}
 public static void main(String[] args) {
        Animal a = new Animal();
        Dog d = new Dog();
        System.out.println(a instanceof Animal); // true
        System.out.println(d instanceof Dog);    // true
        System.out.println(d instanceof Animal); // true (Dog is a subclass of Animal)
        System.out.println(a instanceof Dog);    // false (Animal is not Dog)
    }

