Q24. Difference between Compiler, Interpreter and JIT ?
Compiler :
- It takes your entire program and converts it into machine language (computer can understand) before running it.
- Think of the compiler as a translator for the whole book, After translation, you can run it anytime without translating again.
   eg- javac converts .java → .class.
Interpreter :
- It reads one line at a time and immediately executes it.
- Think of the interpreter as a translator while reading the book,No separate program file is created.
JIT (Just-In-Time Compiler) :
- JVM first starts like an interpreter (line by line).
- Think of JIT as a smart hybrid translator, For parts used a lot (“hot spots”), JIT translates them to machine code to make them run faster.
  eg- loops or frequently used methods.

