Q30. Explain Difference Between Primitive vs Non-Primitive Data Types ?
Primitive :
- They store simple values directly in memory (stack memory usually).
- Number of types: 8    byte, short, int, long, float, double, char, boolean
- can't assign null
Non-Primitive Data Types :
- They store references (addresses) to objects, not the actual data in heap memory (actual object) + reference in stack.
- eg : String, Arrays, Classes, Interfaces, Enums, Objects you create (custom objects)
- can be assign to null to indicate no object reference.




